      <memberdef kind="function" id="namespace_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_hoop_1a2c3bba2015b259961062d36733b18c00" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="yes" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>double</type>
        <definition>double Marmot::Discretizations::Meshfree::Hoop::ringOfFacePoint</definition>
        <argsstring>(const Eigen::Vector2d &amp;Y, const Eigen::Vector2d &amp;center, double deltaF33, const Fastor::Tensor&lt; double, 2, 2 &gt; &amp;deltaF)</argsstring>
        <name>ringOfFacePoint</name>
        <qualifiedname>Marmot::Discretizations::Meshfree::Hoop::ringOfFacePoint</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>const Eigen::Vector2d &amp;</type>
          <declname>Y</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>const Eigen::Vector2d &amp;</type>
          <declname>center</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>double</type>
          <declname>deltaF33</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>const Fastor::Tensor&lt; double, 2, 2 &gt; &amp;</type>
          <declname>deltaF</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>The ring of a point of a face of the particle after the increment, <formula id="68">$ 2\pi x_r(\boldsymbol{Y}) $</formula>. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>The face moves with the motion of the particle,   <formula id="69">$ \boldsymbol{x}(\boldsymbol{Y}) =
\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}
+ \Delta\boldsymbol{F}\,(\boldsymbol{Y} - \bar{\boldsymbol{Y}}) $</formula>, the motion Nanson&apos;s formula of the face assumes, with the center at <formula id="70">$ \bar x_r = \bar r\,\Delta F_{\theta\theta} $</formula>. The ring <formula id="71">$ 2\pi x_r $</formula> is the ring weight <formula id="12">$ 2\pi r $</formula> of the point times its hoop stretch increment <formula id="72">$ x_r / r $</formula>, by which the area of the face grows beyond that of the half plane. For a linear increment it is exact; at the center it is  <formula id="73">$ 2\pi
\bar r\,\Delta F_{\theta\theta} $</formula>. It needs no division by <formula id="11">$ r $</formula>. A point on the axis has the ring 0 for an increment that keeps the axis in place; the axis is no boundary and carries no load. <parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">Y</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The point, in the intermediate configuration. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">center</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The particle center <formula id="16">$ \bar{\boldsymbol{Y}} $</formula>. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">deltaF33</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para><formula id="54">$ \Delta F_{\theta\theta} $</formula> of the particle. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">deltaF</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para><formula id="50">$ \Delta\boldsymbol{F} $</formula> of the particle, in the half plane. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para><formula id="68">$ 2\pi x_r(\boldsymbol{Y}) $</formula>. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
